; A trait DEFAULT body whose scrutinee is a DISPATCHED TRAIT METHOD
; cannot serve two impls, and is refused where it stands.
;
; `checkImplComplete` synthesizes the default into every impl that
; omits the method WITHOUT copying the body's nodes, so two impls at
; two types check ONE AST. For most bodies that is invisible. For this
; one it is not: `(mk x)` is resolved on the shared node for the first
; impl and reused for the second, so the second check reads the first's
; answer and the program is refused - `AX3004 type mismatch: expected
; String, found Int` at the default's own line, in a file where neither
; impl is wrong about anything.
;
; That is a DEFECT, not a rule. A default a trait can hand to only one
; implementation is barely a default, and the fix is to copy the body
; per impl. Nothing here argues against fixing it; this file exists so
; that whoever does has the shape in front of them.
;
; WHAT THIS FILE IS NOT. It is NOT what makes the shared-node hazard
; safe, and an earlier draft of this comment said it was. That was
; wrong, and measurably: a default body whose scrutinee is an ordinary
; generic constructor rather than a dispatched call compiles CLEANLY at
; two types and stamps one binder node twice, with disagreeing type
; names, selected by `impl` declaration order.
; `tests/stdlib/373-shared-default-binder.ax` is that program, and
; `scripts/check-fallible-reclaim.sh` is what holds the stamp to
; refusing both answers rather than taking the last one. The refusal
; below hides nothing; it is simply a second symptom of the same
; sharing, and the cheaper one to see.
;
; The two impls are ordered String-then-Int deliberately. The second
; check is the one that disagrees, so reversing them moves the
; expected/found pair and nothing else - the refusal is about sharing,
; not about either type.
(import Str)

(trait (Sized a) where (
  raw :: (-> a Int)
  size :: (-> a Int) = (lambda (x) (+ (raw x) 1))
))

(impl (Sized String) where (
  (raw (lambda (s) (strLen s)))
))

(impl (Sized Int) where (
  (raw (lambda (n) n))
))

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) (+ (size "abc") (size 41)))
